{
  "_comment": "A STUN binding server as its own deployment: `drt --config this start`. Run TWO of these on separate addresses (the stun1/stun2 pair). One server tells a peer what address it saw; two tell it whether that address CHANGED between vantage points, which is the fact that decides whether hole punching can work at all. A client asking only one server gets an address; a client asking two gets to know whether to trust it.",

  "_program": "This one reads its counters, so it carries a program. `stdlib:stun` is the reader for a deployment that has nothing else to do.",
  "program": { "path": "stun_supervisor.lua" },

  "stun": {
    "_bind": "Unlike the relay and the http listener, this is meant to face the world -- reporting the address the world sees is the entire service -- so bind accordingly, and put it behind the same rate limiting as anything else that answers strangers. 3478 is the IANA STUN port.",
    "bind": "0.0.0.0:3478",

    "_queue": "Where counters land on the supervisor, and how often. The server is stateless, so this is a heartbeat and not an event stream.",
    "queue": "stun_in",
    "report_ms": 10000
  }
}
